tokens\": 0, \"reasoning_tokens\": 0, \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0}, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"audio_tokens\": 0, \"cached_tokens\": 0}}, \"model_provider\": \"openai\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\", \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_86eb24cbce\", \"id\": \"chatcmpl-DvsMUjEeGP5epwgalvSgGqNXO3TmX\", \"service_tier\": \"default\"}, \"run\": null, \"type\": \"LLMResult\"}",
        "output.mime_type": "application/json",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.role": "user",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.content": "You are evaluating whether a research summary accurately reflects its source material.\n\nQUERY: What are the differences between callback-based and auto-instrumentation approaches for LLM observability?\n\nSUMMARY TO EVALUATE:\nThe primary difference between callback-based and auto-instrumentation approaches for LLM observability lies in their implementation and ease of use. Callback-based instrumentation requires developers to manually insert code at specific points in their application to collect data, which can be labor-intensive and error-prone. In contrast, auto-instrumentation automates this process by automatically inserting the necessary code to collect observability data without requiring manual intervention. This makes auto-instrumentation more efficient and less prone to human error, as it reduces the need for developers to modify their codebase extensively to achieve observability.\n\nSOURCE EXCERPTS (ground truth):\n[1] Auto-instrumentation automates the process of inserting the necessary code to collect observability data, reducing the need for manual intervention and making it more efficient and less error-prone compared to callback-based methods.\n\nRate the faithfulness of the summary: what fraction of the factual claims in the\nsummary are explicitly supported by at least one source excerpt above?\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object \u2014 no markdown, no extra text:\n{\"faithfulness\": <float 0.0-1.0>, \"label\": \"grounded\" or \"hallucinated\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}\n",
